  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sierra Leone Emergency Cholera response 2012</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sierra Leone is experiencing a severe cholera outbreak, declared an emergency by its President on August 16. The outbreak arrived in the capital, Freetown, in June, and cases have now been detected in 10 of the country&#x2019;s 13 districts.  As of August 22, there have been close to 12,500 cases nation-wide with 224 deaths, a case fatality rate (CFR) of 1.8%.  Western Area district, which includes the capital, Freetown, has the highest number of cases, reporting nearly half of all cases nationwide. 

Cases still seem to be on the rise, indicating that the epidemic curve may not have yet reached its peak.  Given that the rainy season is currently at its height, there is significant concern that the outbreak will continue to escalate over the coming months and may not begin to subside until after the rains end.  In addition, there is much population movement both within the country and between Sierra Leone and neighboring Guinea, which has also declared an outbreak.

This is an 8 week project which will be implemented through CRS and local Caritas partners and will focus on household hygiene and food safety. These activities will provide people with access to clean water and the awareness and practical tools to protect themselves from infection, thereby curbing the spread of the current cholera outbreak.</narrative>
  </description>
